# Sdist-side twin of the size-driven [tool.setuptools.exclude-package-data]
# entries in this directory's pyproject.toml (exclude-package-data governs
# the wheel only).  The two largest Thompson tables are externalized:
# published as GitHub release assets and staged by `gpuwm fetch-tables`
# under the SHA-256 pins in gpuwm/core/thompson_contract.py.  Without these
# lines the sdist exceeds PyPI's 100 MiB per-file cap on freezeH2O.dat
# alone.
# tests/test_package_data_coverage.py pins this file against the wheel list.
exclude gpuwm_data/data/thompson/tables/freezeH2O.dat
exclude gpuwm_data/data/thompson/tables/qr_acr_qg_V4.dat

# The four CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0 RFMIP reference-result NetCDFs are excluded from
# the WHEEL for licensing, and this file states no rule about them.  It USED
# to claim they were "deliberately NOT excluded here", on the reasoning that
# the sdist is the source repository's own form and a source build must be
# able to run the suite that reads them.  The built artifact disproved it:
# 14 of the 18 files under gpuwm_data/data/rrtmgp/ reach the sdist, the same
# 14 the wheel carries.  `exclude-package-data` governs the SDIST'S DEFAULT
# FILE SET as well as the wheel -- setuptools' sdist folds package-data in
# through the same step -- so an `exclude` written for the wheel takes the
# file out of both, and a comment here cannot put it back.
#
# What that costs, stated rather than left to be discovered: unpacking
# gpuwm_data-<v>.tar.gz gives a tree that cannot run the RFMIP acceptance
# gates, because their reference results are not in it.  The repository is
# where those fixtures live (1.85 MiB, so size is not the reason).  Putting
# them into a published sdist is a licensing decision about redistribution,
# not a packaging tidy-up, so it is left for whoever owns that call.
# tests/test_package_data_coverage.py measures both artifacts and fails if
# the two stop agreeing -- including on the day someone decides to add them.
